# EVERY LAYER THAT DENIES HAVING A REMAINDER, BESIDE WHETHER ITS OWN TWO NUMBERS IMPLY ONE.
# One row per denial, `violates` where the layer states both a demand and a nameplate that
# differ -- so the remainder it says does not exist is derivable from the filing itself.
#
# ⭐⭐ THE RULE: a layer that denies having a remainder is not contradicted by its own figures.
#    If a layer files a demand AND a nameplate, then `r = n - d` is a quantity the document
#    itself supplies and the denial is refuted by the page it is written on. This spans three
#    elements of one layer, which is why no grammar can state it.
#
# ⛔⛔ AND THERE IS NO ESCAPE HERE FOR "THE TWO COINCIDE EXACTLY", WHICH DOES NOT BELONG IN THE
#    PREDICATE. A point nameplate equal to a point demand is `r = 0`, and `Fit` settles that
#    case in prose: "IT IS A CLEARANCE FIT, which is ISO 286's own answer for the line-to-line
#    case, where minimum clearance is zero." A clearance is a REMAINDER, filed with a sign and
#    a quantity of [0, 0, 0]. Exempting it let the wrapper's absence stand in for a filing the
#    schema had already decided, and it was the last place `none` on a remainder could hide.
#
# ⭐ `derived` IS THE ONE DENIAL A STATED PAIR DOES NOT CONTRADICT, and that is what the word
#   means: "this is an OUTPUT, computed from other elements ... the receiver computes it."
#   Filing both figures and declining to restate the difference is the intended use.
#
# ⛔⛔ THE VERSION FIRST PROPOSED FOR THIS RULE WAS VACUOUS BY CONSTRUCTION, and it is worth
#    recording why. It was "a denied remainder names no holders and no absorber" -- but
#    `Holder` and `absorber` live INSIDE the `Remainder` element, so a layer that declined the
#    element cannot carry either. XSD already enforces it structurally and the absorber half is
#    now a CHECK in the DDL. A rule that cannot fail examines nothing however many rows it
#    counts, which is the trap this whole file is written to name.
#
# ⭐ TODAY BOTH DENIALS FILE THEIR NAMEPLATE AS `notApplicable` -- a ratio and an object store,
#   neither of which states a nameplate for a demand to be subtracted from -- so both pass, and
#   the rule is thin rather than vacuous: it examines two real rows and could fail on the next
#   filing.
--
-- pm:StatedRemainder's absent branch against the layer's own pm:Demand and pm:Nameplate.
SELECT r.rule, p.filing, p.layer, p.violates, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(checks/roster.sqlc)
) r
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT d.filing, d.layer,
           (dm.d_low IS NOT NULL AND np.n_low IS NOT NULL
            AND d.reason <> 'derived')                             AS violates,
           CASE WHEN dm.d_low IS NULL OR np.n_low IS NULL
                THEN format('`%s`, and no remainder is derivable: %s',
                            d.reason,
                            CASE WHEN dm.d_low IS NULL THEN 'no demand is stated'
                                 ELSE 'no nameplate is stated' END)
                WHEN d.reason = 'derived'
                THEN format('`derived`, and demand [%s, %s] against a nameplate of [%s, %s] '
                            'is exactly what the receiver computes it from',
                            dm.d_low, dm.d_high, np.n_low, np.n_high)
                ELSE format('`%s`, yet demand [%s, %s] against a nameplate of [%s, %s] '
                            'gives a remainder the filing supplies itself',
                            d.reason, dm.d_low, dm.d_high, np.n_low, np.n_high)
           END AS detail
    FROM      (
        :compose(layers/denied_remainders.sqlc)
    ) d
    LEFT JOIN (
        :compose(layers/demand.sqlc)
    ) dm USING (filing, layer)
    LEFT JOIN (
        :compose(layers/nameplate.sqlc)
    ) np USING (filing, layer)
) p ON true
WHERE r.slug = 'denied_remainder_is_not_contradicted'
